# Source-of-intent for the docling extraction microservice. Loose ranges live
# HERE; the fully-pinned, hashed lock is the generated requirements.txt.
# Regenerate after editing with:
#   pip-compile requirements.in -o requirements.txt --generate-hashes
#
# Kept separate from the Django backend's requirements on purpose: docling
# pulls in PyTorch + layout/table ML models (~2-3 GB), which we do NOT want
# coupled to the API image. This service is its own App Platform component
# (see ../.do/app.yaml). Upper bounds cap each dep below its next major so a
# recompile never crosses a major boundary without a deliberate edit here.
#
# CPU-only PyTorch. App Platform components have no GPU, so the default CUDA
# build of torch (pulled in transitively by docling) only adds ~5 GB of
# unusable nvidia-cu13/cuda-toolkit libraries — enough to overflow the build
# disk and fail the image snapshot. Pinning the +cpu wheels from PyTorch's CPU
# index drops every nvidia-* dep with zero runtime cost (no GPU = the CUDA
# build already ran on CPU). pip-compile emits this --extra-index-url into the
# generated lock so the Docker build can resolve the +cpu wheels. To bump
# torch, re-resolve the matching torch/torchvision +cpu pair from that index.
--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
torch==2.12.0+cpu
torchvision==0.27.0+cpu
docling>=2.121.0,<3
# ASGI app + server. python-multipart is intentionally omitted — /extract
# takes the raw request body (filename via the X-Filename header), so there's
# no multipart parsing on this side.
fastapi>=0.141.1,<1
uvicorn[standard]>=0.52.4,<1
